 # Tetrahedral representation and traversal
-Also called: Projected Tetrahedra family, Tetrahedral Volume Rendering
+
+Also called: Projected Tetrahedra, Cell Projection with Tetrahedra
 
 ## Overview
-This family covers methods that decompose volumetric data into tetrahedral primitives to enable efficient rendering or surface extraction. It solves the problem of handling unstructured or irregular grids (e.g., CFD data) and resolving topological ambiguities in regular grids by leveraging the planar faces and consistent topology of tetrahedra [S2, unofficial][S3][S7]. Unlike [Marching Cubes](940-marching-tetrahedrons-algorithm.md) which suffers from directional ambiguities, or [Ray Marching](877-projected-tetrahedra.md) which is computationally expensive for unstructured data, this family leverages hardware-accelerated rasterization (via projection) or exact analytic extraction (via Delaunay/SDF structures) [S3][S5]. It differs from [Tetrahedral Strips and Fans](1389-tetrahedral-strips-and-fans.md) by focusing on general classification and decomposition rather than optimized connectivity for stripification.
+This family covers methods that decompose volumetric data into tetrahedral primitives, project them into screen space (or traverse them in object space), and rasterize or ray-march the resulting geometry to render the volume. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering unstructured or irregular grids (e.g., CFD data) by leveraging hardware-accelerated rasterization or optimized spatial traversal, avoiding the sampling artifacts and performance bottlenecks of regular grid marching [S3][S5]. Unlike [Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm](940-marching-tetrahedrons-algorithm.md), which extracts isosurfaces, this family renders the volume directly via translucent polygons or ray intersections [S11, unofficial].
 
